<article class="env-definition" id="01SR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01SR">Definition <span data-tag="01SR">29.14.1</span>.</a> Let $P$ be a property of ring maps. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say that $P$ is <em>local</em> if the following hold: 
</p>
<ol>
  <li><p>
For any ring map $R \to A$, and any $f \in R$ we have $P(R \to A) \Rightarrow P(R_ f \to A_ f)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For any rings $R$, $A$, any $f \in R$, $a\in A$, and any ring map $R_ f \to A$ we have $P(R_ f \to A) \Rightarrow P(R \to A_ a)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For any ring map $R \to A$, and $a_ i \in A$ such that $(a_1, \ldots , a_ n) = A$ then $\forall i, P(R \to A_{a_ i}) \Rightarrow P(R \to A)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li><li><p>
We say that $P$ is <em>stable under base change</em> if for any ring maps $R \to A$, $R \to R'$ we have $P(R \to A) \Rightarrow P(R' \to R' \otimes _ R A)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $P$ is <em>stable under composition</em> if for any ring maps $A \to B$, $B \to C$ we have $P(A \to B) \wedge P(B \to C) \Rightarrow P(A \to C)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>